WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.892
[MUSIC PLAYIN]

00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:11.600
MATTHEW O'CONNOR: Defending
the world's largest network

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:13.760
against constantly-evolving
cyber threats

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:17.150
has driven us to develop
advanced tools to help keep us

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:18.590
and our customers ahead.

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:22.340
I'm going to go into detail on
four ways we can help defend

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:24.920
your business critical data
and applications from some

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:27.360
of today's biggest
security threats.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.330
Let's start with ransomware.

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:31.550
Recent Google research
shows cyber thieves

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:34.730
have made at least $25 million
from ransomware in the last two

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:35.270
years.

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:37.640
And this is expected to grow.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:39.320
To protect ourselves
and our customers

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:41.250
from this and other
types of malware,

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:44.270
we've built multiple layers
of protection, most of which

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:47.420
are automated and available
to our customers by default.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:50.870
First, our global visibility in
the malicious sites and content

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:53.150
makes detection
highly effective.

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:54.710
If you're a Gmail
user, we prevent

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:56.390
thousands of
malicious attachments

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:58.130
from ever reaching inboxes.

00:00:58.130 --> 00:00:59.930
If something does
reach your inbox,

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:01.700
Google Safe Browsing
in our Chrome browser

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:05.180
protects users by identifying
dangerous links in email

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:08.050
and showing warnings
if users click on them.

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:09.740
This stops access
to any websites

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:11.960
which are known to
distribute malware.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:14.090
If you've ever been blocked
from a site like this,

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:16.550
you've experienced
Safe Browsing at work.

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:19.460
Every minute of the day, we find
and label another malware site

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:24.000
and warn users of suspected
malware hundreds of times.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.600
In Google Drive,
all files undergo

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:29.780
a malware scan prior to
any download or sharing.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:33.260
Drive also stores files in
non-executable formats, which

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:35.200
prevent ransomware
from propagating.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.580
Our Chrome operating
system continually

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:40.250
updates automatically,
ensuring it's highly resistant

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:42.380
to malware compromise.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:44.510
Applications are
sandboxed to prevent harm

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:46.370
to the host machine.

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:48.110
Often, these features
remove any need

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:51.270
for anti-malware or
anti-virus software.

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.510
And at the mobile device
level, every single day,

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:56.940
we check the health of over
400 million Android devices

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:59.750
and scan over 6 billion
mobile applications downloaded

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.960
to Android phones to make
sure they're not infected.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:05.520
Safe Browsing protects over
2 billion mobile phones,

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:07.550
laptops, tablets, and
other devices which

00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:09.960
use Google software.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:12.720
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:15.070
Now let's look at denial
of service attacks.

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:17.760
A Distributed Denial of
Service attack, or DDoS attack,

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.560
is an attempt to make
an online service

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:21.720
unavailable by overwhelming
it with traffic

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:23.220
from multiple sources.

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:26.070
It can have a huge impact if
businesses aren't adequately

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:27.300
prepared.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:30.570
As a Google Cloud customer,
you are protected by default

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:33.480
to this type of attack, as the
scale of our infrastructure

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.820
enables us to simply
absorb many of them.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:38.430
For context, a huge
attack last year

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:41.160
had a strength of around
1 terabit per second.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.710
The whole internet has
a bisection bandwidth

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:45.987
of 200 terabits per second.

00:02:45.987 --> 00:02:48.070
Now, when you compare this
to a single Google Data

00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:51.780
Center, which has a
bisection bandwidth of $1,300

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:54.510
terabits per second,
you can see we

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:57.550
have built-in level of internal
capacity multiple times

00:02:57.550 --> 00:02:59.700
that of any traffic
load we anticipate.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:01.350
When there is an
attack, we have time

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.160
to isolate it and address it.

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:04.500
But we don't stop there.

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:06.360
In Google Cloud
Platform, customers

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.730
benefit directly from our
central DDoS mitigation

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:12.490
service that provides additional
multi-tier, multi-layer

00:03:12.490 --> 00:03:14.550
protections and further
reduces the risk

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:17.190
to services running behind
our Google front ends.

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:20.880
When the system detects
an attack is taking place,

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.610
it can configure load balancers
to drop or throttle traffic

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:26.900
associated with the attack.

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:29.190
So what do we do to
prevent data breaches?

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:31.130
It's critical to know
where sensitive data is.

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:33.682
But that can be difficult
in complex systems.

00:03:33.682 --> 00:03:35.390
At Google, we encrypt
our customers' data

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:38.354
in different ways, depending
upon what the data is doing.

00:03:38.354 --> 00:03:39.770
And all of this
happens by default

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:42.620
with no user action required.

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:45.320
Data is potentially vulnerable
to unauthorized access

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:48.650
as it travels across the
internet or within networks.

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:50.690
Because it's linked to
most ISPs in the world,

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:52.370
Google's private
global network helps

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:54.530
to improve the security
of data in transit

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:57.020
by limiting the hops
across the public internet.

00:03:57.020 --> 00:03:58.940
Any data traveling from
a customer's device

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:03.800
to Google's Cloud is
protected using HTTPS or TLS,

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:06.620
limiting opportunities for
traffic to be intercepted.

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:08.960
In fact, Google was the
first major cloud provider

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:10.630
to enable this by default.

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:12.380
And Google, we encrypt
our customers' data

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:15.900
in different ways, depending
upon what the data is doing.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:19.010
Whether it is stored in
a database or in transit

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:21.440
to and from Google, it
all happens by default

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:24.110
with no user action required.

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:26.090
Data is encrypted at
the hardware layer

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:27.800
inside of our data center.

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.600
That way, it can
only be decrypted

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:32.510
in our cloud on another
verified Google machine.

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:34.430
When it's stored,
data is broken up

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:37.660
into numerous different shards
and sent to different servers.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:39.500
This guards against
hackers and is

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:42.860
good for disaster recovery and
business continuity, insuring

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:45.350
against natural disasters,
unplanned down times

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:47.060
or equipment failures.

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:50.430
When you call up a document
in Google Drive, for example,

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:52.830
the document is recalled from
all of its storage points,

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.640
decrypted, and reassembled
in the blink of an eye.

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:58.070
Additionally, as customers,
you have the option

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:00.740
of managing encryption keys
with our Cloud Key Management

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:03.830
Service, or even supplying
your own encryption keys

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:07.790
if you're looking for additional
granular levels of control.

00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:10.270
Data loss prevention is
an effective technology

00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:12.770
to ensure that sensitive data
doesn't leave the organization

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:15.430
or get accessed by people
that it's not intended for.

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:17.570
In Google Cloud
Platform and G Suite,

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:20.610
users can find and
secure sensitive content,

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:23.870
minimize what data they collect,
and implement data governance

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:24.810
policies.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:27.740
Our DLP API helps you to
manage and run analytics

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:30.200
on your Cloud data without
introducing additional risk

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:31.610
to your organization.

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:34.820
It provides fast,
scalable classification

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:38.030
and optional redaction for
over 40 sensitive data types,

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:41.810
like credit card numbers, names,
passport numbers, and more.

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:44.930
As you can see here, it can be
used to automatically classify

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:47.840
or redact sensitive data from a
text stream before you write it

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:51.620
to disk, generate logs,
or perform analysis on it.

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:54.140
Finally, let's look at an attack
that many organizations are

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:55.140
concerned about--

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:56.810
phishing.

00:05:56.810 --> 00:06:00.470
According to the 2017 Verizon
Data Breach Investigations

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:03.530
Report, 90% of all
incidents and breaches

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:05.810
that involve social
actions by external actors

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:07.580
include phishing.

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:09.560
Everyone is
overwhelmed with email.

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:11.390
And it just takes one
person to hurriedly

00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:14.510
click on a rogue message to
have a phishing incident.

00:06:14.510 --> 00:06:16.700
If you've ever seen an
email that looks like this,

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:18.500
you'll know what
I'm talking about.

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:20.600
Our first defense is to
prevent phishing emails

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:22.780
from ever reaching people.

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:26.100
Gmail filtering blocks the
majority of malicious email.

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:27.920
And our global
visibility across users

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:30.340
makes this visibility
highly effective.

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:32.620
Gmail also restricts
the use of file types

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:34.940
that carry a high potential
for security risks.

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:37.410
Even inside a compressed
file, if a malicious link

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:39.200
gets through, the next
layer of protection

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:41.540
is Safe Browsing in Chrome,
which prevents users

00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:43.790
from visiting known
phishing websites where

00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:46.160
their credentials can be
stolen or malware installed

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:47.330
on their device.

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:50.210
Then, even if an attacker
manages to gain the credentials

00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:53.180
they were looking for, such
as a username and password,

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:55.790
we provide two-factor
verification and security

00:06:55.790 --> 00:06:58.130
keys, which make the use
of compromised credentials

00:06:58.130 --> 00:06:59.810
extremely difficult.

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:01.280
Instead of entering
a unique code

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:03.900
as the second factor at
sign-in, Security Key

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:06.470
sends us cryptographic
proof that user's

00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:09.650
on a legitimate Google site and
that they have their security

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:11.060
keys with them.

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:12.710
A physical action is required.

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:14.810
And since most
hijackers are remote,

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:17.300
their efforts can be thwarted,
because they cannot get

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:19.310
possession of the security key.

00:07:19.310 --> 00:07:21.170
To increase control
further, businesses

00:07:21.170 --> 00:07:23.660
can take advantage of Security
Key Enforcement, which

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:26.030
allows Google Cloud
administrators to enable

00:07:26.030 --> 00:07:30.050
two-step verification that
forces users to use security

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:32.690
keys as the second factor.

00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:35.930
Security keys work with
Google accounts at no charge.

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:38.600
But you'll need to buy a
compatible USB device directly

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:40.880
from a U2F-participating vendor.

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:43.730
Finally, if a Chromebook
somehow gets compromised

00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:45.620
as a result of a
phishing attack,

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:48.650
it can quickly and easily be
restored to a known good state.

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:51.290
As you can see, we're constantly
innovating for our customers

00:07:51.290 --> 00:07:53.900
to help prevent compromises
all across the stack.

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:56.810
Compared to legacy security
approaches, using Google Cloud

00:07:56.810 --> 00:08:00.110
means that typical deployment
is faster and scale

00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:01.640
much more easily.

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:03.230
Automation is easier.

00:08:03.230 --> 00:08:05.720
Functionality evolves and
improves more quickly.

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:08.330
And often, the cost of
acquisition is lower.

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:10.741
